<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acre War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Acrean Revolution · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> War was a conflict over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>-rich region of Acre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, then claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> but settled mainly by Brazilian rubber tappers. Brazilian settlers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/placido-de-castro">José Plácido de Castro</a> rose against Bolivian rule and defeated its forces in 1902–1903. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-petropolis">Treaty of Petrópolis</a> of 1903 Bolivia ceded Acre to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in return for money and a railway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acre-war.jpg" alt="Acre War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathan.rpk · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fotos_da_Guerra_do_Acre.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> had been set by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> of 1867, which left the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, about five times the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, to Bolivia. Remote and thought to be of little value, it was home to a small number of Indians and a handful of Brazilians and Bolivians. When the price of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> rose sharply in the late 19th century, between 15,000 and 60,000 adventurers and settlers, most of them from Brazil, moved in to tap the rubber trees, travelling by river steamer, canoe and raft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 2 January 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> opened a customs office at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-alonso">Puerto Alonso</a>, angering the Brazilian settlers. A lawyer, José de Carvalho, led a revolt on 30 April 1899, and on 14 July the Spaniard Luis Gálvez Rodríguez de Arias, backed by the officials of Amazonas, proclaimed a Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. A Brazilian flotilla dissolved it on 15 March 1900, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> still held Acre to be Bolivian. A second republic, founded by the so-called Expedition of Poets in November 1900, was defeated by the Bolivians at Puerto Alonso around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>.</p><p>In 1901 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> leased almost total control of the province for 30 years to the Bolivian Syndicate, a company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and British capitalists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> objected, and Brazil closed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>'s tributaries to Bolivian trade. In 1902 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> tappers turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/placido-de-castro">José Plácido de Castro</a>, who took the Bolivian depot at Xapuri on 6 August. Beaten by a Bolivian battalion under Colonel Rosendo Rojas on 18 September, he raised a force of about a thousand men, won a series of fights and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-alonso">Puerto Alonso</a>, whose garrison surrendered on 24 January 1903. The Third Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, proclaimed three days later, had the support of President Rodrigues Alves and his foreign minister, the Baron of Rio Branco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A Bolivian force under President José Manuel Pando arrived but saw no major fighting before a preliminary treaty was signed on 21 March 1903. By the Treaty of Petrópolis, agreed that November, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> gave up about 191,000 square kilometres in exchange for land from Mato Grosso, 2 million pounds and the promise of the Madeira-Mamoré Railroad; the Bolivian Syndicate received 110,000 pounds. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> became Brazilian territory in 1904, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/placido-de-castro">Plácido de Castro</a> its first president. The war showed the advantage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> drew from lying downstream on rivers that the Bolivians could reach only across the Andes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acre War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
